IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `scta-jurisdiction`
IMPORT `scta-orders-and-appeal`

-- Small Claims Tribunals Act 1984 -- worked cases.
--
-- Claims about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. The money
-- figures come from s 2(1) of the Act itself, so unlike the Employment Claims
-- Act row the limits here are not invented; but no order under s 2(1) varying
-- them was retrieved, and one may exist.

§ `Fixtures`

GIVEN `relates to a contract` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the nature` IS A NatureOfContractClaim,
      `contract value` IS A NUMBER,
      `quantum` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A ClaimValue
DECIDE `value` `relates to a contract` `the nature` `contract value` `quantum` IS
    ClaimValue WITH
        `the claim relates to a contract` IS `relates to a contract`
        `the nature of the contract claim` IS `the nature`
        `the value of the contract, in dollars` IS `contract value`
        `the quantum of the claim, in dollars` IS `quantum`

GIVEN `the subject` IS A ClaimSubject,
      `the value` IS A ClaimValue,
      `served here` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `motor accident damage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `State Courts cannot hear it` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `years since accrual` IS A NUMBER,
      `memorandum` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `abandoned` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Claim
DECIDE `a claim` `the subject` `the value` `served here` `motor accident damage` `State Courts cannot hear it` `years since accrual` `memorandum` `abandoned` IS
    Claim WITH
        `the subject` IS `the subject`
        `the value` IS `the value`
        `served in Singapore on the respondent under section 19(1)` IS `served here`
        `in respect of damage to property by an accident involving the use of a motor vehicle` IS `motor accident damage`
        `the State Courts have no jurisdiction over it` IS `State Courts cannot hear it`
        `years since the cause of action accrued` IS `years since accrual`
        `the parties have agreed the extended limit by a memorandum signed by them` IS `memorandum`
        `the claimant has abandoned the excess under section 9` IS `abandoned`

-- A plain consumer claim: $3,000 for a sofa that was never delivered.
`a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 3000 3000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- The same claim, three years old.
`a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 3000 3000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 3 FALSE FALSE

-- Exactly two years, and a day over.
`a $3,000 claim, exactly two years old` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 3000 3000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 2 FALSE FALSE

-- A $25,000 claim, in the band between the two limits, with and without the
-- memorandum.
`a $25,000 services claim with a memorandum` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 25000 25000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 TRUE FALSE
`a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 25000 25000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE
`a $25,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 25000 25000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE TRUE

-- The band, three years old, with a memorandum. This is where the two readings
-- of s 5 part company.
`a $25,000 services claim, three years old, with a memorandum` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 25000 25000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 3 TRUE FALSE

-- A $40,000 claim: above the extended limit, so neither route helps except
-- abandonment.
`a $40,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 40000 40000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE TRUE

-- s 2(2)(a): rescission of a $90,000 renovation contract, with a $4,000 deposit
-- to come back.
`rescission of a $90,000 renovation contract` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `rescission of the contract` 90000 4000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- The same facts, pleaded as a debt for the $4,000 instead.
`the same $4,000 claimed as a debt` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 90000 4000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- s 2(2)(b): a $6,000 progress payment under the same $90,000 contract.
`a $6,000 progress payment under a $90,000 contract` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `recovery of a progress payment due under the contract` 90000 6000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- s 5(2)(a): a dented car.
`a $2,000 claim for a dented car` MEANS
    `a claim` `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
        (`value` FALSE `anything else` 0 2000) TRUE TRUE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- The same amount of damage to something that is not a motor vehicle accident:
-- a contractor who put a hole in a wall.
`a $2,000 claim for a damaged wall` MEANS
    `a claim` `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
        (`value` FALSE `anything else` 0 2000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- s 5(1)(b): a respondent who cannot be served here.
`a $3,000 claim against a respondent who cannot be served here` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 3000 3000) FALSE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- Schedule para 1(b) and the Community Disputes Resolution Act carve-out.
`a neighbour dispute within section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act` MEANS
    `a claim` `a claim mentioned in section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act 2015`
        (`value` FALSE `anything else` 0 2000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- Schedule para 1(c): a two-year residential lease, and a three-year one.
`unpaid rent under a two-year residential lease` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the lease of residential premises not exceeding 2 years`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 24000 500) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE
`unpaid rent under a three-year residential lease` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract for the lease of residential premises exceeding 2 years`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 36000 500) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- Schedule para 2: the money-changer.
`a $1,500 claim against a licensed money-changer` MEANS
    `a claim` `a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes with a licensed money-changer`
        (`value` TRUE `anything else` 1500 1500) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- A Town Council collecting.
`a Town Council recovering $800 of charges` MEANS
    `a claim` `a Town Council recovering a charge, fee, expense or penalty`
        (`value` FALSE `anything else` 0 800) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

-- Something the Schedule does not name: a claim for defamation.
`a $5,000 defamation claim` MEANS
    `a claim` `something the Schedule does not specify`
        (`value` FALSE `anything else` 0 5000) TRUE FALSE FALSE 1 FALSE FALSE

§ `The Schedule -- what is a specified claim`

#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a contract for the lease of residential premises not exceeding 2 years`
#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a Town Council recovering a charge, fee, expense or penalty`

-- The three things the Schedule shuts out.
#ASSERT NOT `this is a specified claim` `a claim mentioned in section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act 2015`
#ASSERT NOT `this is a specified claim` `a contract for the lease of residential premises exceeding 2 years`
#ASSERT NOT `this is a specified claim` `something the Schedule does not specify`

-- Schedule para 2 brings the money-changer in through para 1(a).
#ASSERT `the claim is within Schedule paragraph 1(a)` `a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes with a licensed money-changer`
#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a contract to buy or sell foreign currency notes with a licensed money-changer`

-- Six of the nine limbs are there for an institution to collect from an
-- individual, and three are for the individual.
#ASSERT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `a management corporation recovering a contribution or interest under that Act`
#ASSERT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `the Housing and Development Board recovering an improvement contribution, interest or penalty`
#ASSERT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `the Council of the Singapore Business Federation recovering a subscription`
#ASSERT NOT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `a contract for the sale of goods or the provision of services`
#ASSERT NOT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
#ASSERT NOT `this limb of the Schedule exists for an institution to collect` `a contract for the lease of residential premises not exceeding 2 years`

§ `Section 2(2) -- the value of a contract claim is not always the sum sought`

#ASSERT `the value of the claim` (`value` TRUE `anything else` 90000 4000) EQUALS 4000

-- s 2(2)(a): on a rescission claim the value is the value of the CONTRACT.
#ASSERT `the value of the claim` (`value` TRUE `rescission of the contract` 90000 4000) EQUALS 90000

-- s 2(2)(b): and on a progress-payment claim, likewise.
#ASSERT `the value of the claim` (`value` TRUE `recovery of a progress payment due under the contract` 90000 6000) EQUALS 90000

-- So the same $4,000 is inside the tribunal pleaded as a debt and outside it
-- pleaded as rescission.
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `the same $4,000 claimed as a debt` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `rescission of a $90,000 renovation contract` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $6,000 progress payment under a $90,000 contract` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- And there is nothing to abandon, because what exceeds the limit is the value
-- of the contract and not the sum sought.
#ASSERT `there is an excess the claimant could abandon under section 9` `rescission of a $90,000 renovation contract` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `abandoning the sum sought would bring the value within the limit` `rescission of a $90,000 renovation contract`
#ASSERT NOT `abandoning the sum sought would bring the value within the limit` `a $6,000 progress payment under a $90,000 contract`
#ASSERT `abandoning the sum sought would bring the value within the limit` `the same $4,000 claimed as a debt`

§ `Section 5 -- jurisdiction`

#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $1,500 claim against a licensed money-changer` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a Town Council recovering $800 of charges` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $5,000 defamation claim` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a neighbour dispute within section 4 of the Community Disputes Resolution Act` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `unpaid rent under a three-year residential lease` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- s 5(1)(b): service in Singapore is a condition of jurisdiction, not of
-- procedure.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(1) is satisfied` `a $3,000 claim against a respondent who cannot be served here`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $3,000 claim against a respondent who cannot be served here` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- s 5(2)(a): THE MOTOR VEHICLE HOLE. The commonest small property claim there
-- is, excluded from the tribunal while identical damage from any other cause is
-- inside it.
#ASSERT `this is a specified claim` `a tort claim for damage caused to property`
#ASSERT `section 5(2) excludes the claim` `a $2,000 claim for a dented car`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $2,000 claim for a dented car` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $2,000 claim for a damaged wall` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- s 5(3)(b): two years from accrual. "After the expiry of 2 years", so two years
-- exactly is still in.
#ASSERT `the claim is within the two-year period in section 5(3)(b)` `a $3,000 claim, exactly two years old`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $3,000 claim, exactly two years old` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the claim is within the two-year period in section 5(3)(b)` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- And s 46 is no help to the stale claim here, because s 46 is confined to a
-- claim that IS within the tribunal's jurisdiction. The claimant's route to the
-- ordinary courts, where the general six-year period runs, does not come from
-- s 46 at all.
#ASSERT `section 46 preserves an election to go elsewhere` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `section 46 preserves an election to go elsewhere` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

§ `Sections 5(4) and 9 -- the two ways past the prescribed limit`

#ASSERT NOT `the value is within the prescribed limit` `a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the value is within the prescribed extended limit` `a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- s 5(4) needs the respondent's signature. Without it the claim is out, and the
-- claimant's only route is to give up $5,000.
#ASSERT `section 5(4) applies to the claim` `a $25,000 services claim with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $25,000 services claim with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(4) applies to the claim` `a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- s 9(1): abandonment needs nobody's agreement.
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $25,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the amount abandoned if the claimant abandons the excess` `a $25,000 services claim with no memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)` EQUALS 5000

-- And abandonment works at any size, which the memorandum does not: a $40,000
-- claim is above the extended limit, so s 5(4) could not reach it even with the
-- respondent's signature, and abandoning $20,000 brings it in.
#ASSERT NOT `the value is within the prescribed extended limit` `a $40,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $40,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the amount abandoned if the claimant abandons the excess` `a $40,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)` EQUALS 20000

§ `THE READING QUESTION -- is section 5(4) subject to the two-year bar?`

-- s 5(3) opens "Except where this Act expressly provides otherwise". s 5(4) is
-- expressed to be "Subject to subsections (1) and (2)" -- and not to subsection
-- (3).
--
-- On the literal reading, a memorandum under s 5(4) confers jurisdiction over a
-- claim in the $20,000-to-$30,000 band whatever its age. On the conservative
-- reading, the two-year bar in s 5(3)(b) applies to every claim.
--
-- The encoding's main function `the tribunal has jurisdiction` takes the
-- CONSERVATIVE reading. The pair below is the only place the two diverge, and it
-- is asserted so that the choice is on the record.
#ASSERT `the two readings of section 5 disagree about this claim` `a $25,000 services claim, three years old, with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT `the tribunal has jurisdiction on the literal reading of section 5(4)` `a $25,000 services claim, three years old, with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction` `a $25,000 services claim, three years old, with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

-- The readings agree everywhere else, including on the stale claim that is
-- inside the prescribed limit -- which is the absurdity the literal reading
-- produces: the larger claim would be heard and the smaller would not.
#ASSERT NOT `the two readings of section 5 disagree about this claim` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal has jurisdiction on the literal reading of section 5(4)` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods, three years old` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the two readings of section 5 disagree about this claim` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `the two readings of section 5 disagree about this claim` `a $25,000 services claim with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

§ `Section 6 -- the gap in the way out`

-- s 6(2): the tribunal is shut if another court has the claim.
#ASSERT `section 6(2) bars the claim from the tribunal` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `section 6(2) bars the claim from the tribunal` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(2) bars the claim from the tribunal` FALSE FALSE

-- s 6(1): once lodged here, nowhere else -- with two exceptions.
#ASSERT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` TRUE `it is still live` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` TRUE `it is still live` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` FALSE `it is still live` FALSE

-- s 6(1)(b): withdrawal and abandonment are the two exits.
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` TRUE `the claimant withdrew it under section 34` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` TRUE `the claimant abandoned it` FALSE

-- THE GAP. A tribunal-ordered discontinuance under s 5(5) is neither a
-- withdrawal nor an abandonment, so s 6(1) still bars every other court. The
-- claimant who lodged in the wrong forum and let the tribunal say so is left
-- with nowhere to go on the words of the Act.
#ASSERT `section 6(1) bars proceedings in another court` TRUE `the tribunal discontinued it under section 5(5) as beyond its jurisdiction` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the claimant can still take the claim to another court` TRUE `the tribunal discontinued it under section 5(5) as beyond its jurisdiction` FALSE TRUE

-- The two cures, both of which have to happen before the discontinuance.
#ASSERT `the claimant can still take the claim to another court` TRUE `the claimant withdrew it under section 34` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `the claimant can still take the claim to another court` TRUE `the tribunal transferred it to another court under section 7` FALSE TRUE

-- s 34 is unqualified, which is what makes the first cure available at all.
#ASSERT `a claimant may withdraw at any time`

§ `Sections 8 and 10 -- division, and the counterclaim nobody moved`

#ASSERT `section 8 forbids the division` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `section 8 forbids the division` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `section 8 forbids the division` FALSE TRUE

-- s 10(4): where nobody applies to move an out-of-limit counterclaim, the
-- tribunal has jurisdiction over it "despite any other provisions of this Act".
-- A respondent with a very large counterclaim who says nothing hands the
-- tribunal jurisdiction over the whole of it.
#ASSERT `section 10(4) gives the tribunal jurisdiction over the counterclaim` TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `section 10(4) gives the tribunal jurisdiction over the counterclaim` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `section 10(4) gives the tribunal jurisdiction over the counterclaim` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `section 10(4) gives the tribunal jurisdiction over the counterclaim` FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `Section 35 -- the orders, and the one that is not capped`

#ASSERT `this order is available on this claim` `money` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` FALSE
#ASSERT `this order is available on this claim` `a work order` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` FALSE
#ASSERT `this order is available on this claim` `costs` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` FALSE

-- s 35(1)(g): vacant possession, only on a Schedule para 1(c) claim and only for
-- unpaid rent. Within that, the Small Claims Tribunal can evict a residential
-- tenant.
#ASSERT `this order is available on this claim` `delivery of vacant possession` `unpaid rent under a two-year residential lease` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `this order is available on this claim` `delivery of vacant possession` `unpaid rent under a two-year residential lease` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `this order is available on this claim` `delivery of vacant possession` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` TRUE

-- s 35(2) caps the money order and the work order and nothing else. The
-- possession order has no "total value" in the subsection's sense, so a tenant
-- can be ordered out of premises of any value on a $500 rent claim.
#ASSERT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `money`
#ASSERT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `a work order`
#ASSERT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `a work order with money in default`
#ASSERT NOT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `delivery of vacant possession`
#ASSERT NOT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `costs`
#ASSERT NOT `section 35(2) caps this kind of order` `an ancillary order`

-- The ceiling, and the fact that abandoning the excess does not raise it.
#ASSERT `the ceiling on a money or work order` `a $3,000 claim for undelivered goods` `the limits stated in section 2(1)` EQUALS 20000
#ASSERT `the ceiling on a money or work order` `a $25,000 services claim with a memorandum` `the limits stated in section 2(1)` EQUALS 30000
#ASSERT `the ceiling on a money or work order` `a $25,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)` EQUALS 20000
#ASSERT `abandoning the excess leaves the ceiling at the prescribed limit` `a $25,000 services claim with the excess abandoned` `the limits stated in section 2(1)`

§ `Section 35(3) -- fourteen days to fix a slip, and no extension`

GIVEN `days` IS A NUMBER,
      `clerical` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `slip` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `miscalculation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `form` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `decided wrongly` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Correction
DECIDE `correction` `days` `clerical` `slip` `miscalculation` `form` `decided wrongly` IS
    Correction WITH
        `days since the order was made` IS `days`
        `a clerical mistake` IS `clerical`
        `an error arising from an accidental slip or omission` IS `slip`
        `a material miscalculation of figures or mistake in a description` IS `miscalculation`
        `a defect of form` IS `form`
        `the complaint is that the tribunal decided the case wrongly` IS `decided wrongly`

`a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 10` MEANS `correction` 10 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 14` MEANS `correction` 14 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 20` MEANS `correction` 20 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a complaint that the tribunal got it wrong, on day 3` MEANS `correction` 3 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE

#ASSERT `the tribunal may correct the order` `a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 10`
#ASSERT `the tribunal may correct the order` `a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 14`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal may correct the order` `a miscalculated figure, spotted on day 20`
#ASSERT NOT `the tribunal may correct the order` `a complaint that the tribunal got it wrong, on day 3`
#ASSERT NOT `the fourteen days in section 35(3) may be extended`

§ `Sections 38, 40, 41 and 42 -- afterwards`

GIVEN `law` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `facts` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a section 41 order` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `aggrieved` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `months` IS A NUMBER,
      `further period allowed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Challenge
DECIDE `challenge` `law` `jurisdiction` `facts` `a section 41 order` `aggrieved` `months` `further period allowed` IS
    Challenge WITH
        `the ground involves a question of law` IS `law`
        `the ground is that the claim was outside the tribunal's jurisdiction` IS `jurisdiction`
        `the ground is that the tribunal found the facts wrongly` IS `facts`
        `the order is one section 41(1) names` IS `a section 41 order`
        `the applicant is a person aggrieved by the order` IS `aggrieved`
        `months since the order was made` IS `months`
        `the tribunal has allowed a further period` IS `further period allowed`

`a point of law` MEANS `challenge` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE
`a jurisdiction point` MEANS `challenge` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE
`the tribunal believed the wrong witness` MEANS `challenge` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE
`an order made in the party's absence, one month later` MEANS `challenge` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 1 FALSE
`an order made in the party's absence, three months later` MEANS `challenge` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 3 FALSE
`the same, with a further period allowed` MEANS `challenge` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 3 TRUE
`an order made in the party's presence, one month later` MEANS `challenge` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE 1 FALSE

#ASSERT `an appeal lies under section 38` `a point of law`
#ASSERT `an appeal lies under section 38` `a jurisdiction point`

-- s 40: there is no appeal on the facts.
#ASSERT NOT `an appeal lies under section 38` `the tribunal believed the wrong witness`
#ASSERT NOT `an appeal lies on a question of fact`
#ASSERT NOT `the party has some way of challenging the order` `the tribunal believed the wrong witness`

-- s 41 is a remedy for not having been there, not for having lost. One month,
-- extendable.
#ASSERT `the order may be set aside under section 41` `an order made in the party's absence, one month later`
#ASSERT NOT `the order may be set aside under section 41` `an order made in the party's absence, three months later`
#ASSERT `the order may be set aside under section 41` `the same, with a further period allowed`
#ASSERT NOT `the order may be set aside under section 41` `an order made in the party's presence, one month later`
#ASSERT `the party has some way of challenging the order` `an order made in the party's absence, one month later`

-- s 36 and s 42: enforcement is a District Court's, and an appeal does not stop
-- it.
#ASSERT `the order is enforceable as a District Court judgment` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the order is enforceable as a District Court judgment` FALSE
#ASSERT `the order may be enforced while an appeal is pending` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `the order may be enforced while an appeal is pending` TRUE
